+# Daily Grind origin transfer
+
+This repository initially contained only a static page: no existing manifest, account backend, IndexedDB stores, or preference keys. The migration code therefore supplies a configurable deployment pair. It does not move an already installed app until both bundles are deployed with its real identity.
+
+1. Fill in `oldOrigin` and `newOrigin` in `migration.config.json`. Both must be HTTPS origins on the same registrable domain. The build uses the Public Suffix List (including private suffixes) to reject unrelated sites. `OLD_ORIGIN` and `NEW_ORIGIN` can override these values during a build.
+2. Set `oldId` to the **exact existing manifest ID**, resolved against its origin. For a legacy manifest without an explicit ID, use its resolved `start_url`. Set `newId` to a stable destination identity. The `/` defaults are assumptions, not discovered installed identities. Preserve the existing app's name, icons, start URL, scope, and manifest shortcuts in `manifest.json` before building; this repository has no previous manifest to recover them from. The supplied start URL and scope assume root-hosted apps.
+3. Inventory real preference storage and list only its localStorage keys in `localStorageKeys`. `daily-grind:preferences` is a proposed key, not an existing setting found in this page. Never add authentication tokens or session data. Cookies, IndexedDB, service-worker caches and browser permissions are **not** copied by this implementation; add an app-specific data adapter or server-side account sync if the installed app uses them. Users sign in and grant permissions again as needed.
+4. Run `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and `pnpm build`. Deploy `dist/new` to the new origin, then `dist/old` to the old origin. Both origins must remain under your control and use the same configuration. This project has no deployment credentials or hosting configuration.
+
+The new manifest claims the predecessor with `migrate_from`; the old manifest signals `migrate_to`. The old bundle serves `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` with the exact destination app ID as its key. Keep that extensionless file accessible as JSON with no authentication or redirect, and make manifest/config responses revalidate during the rollout. Ensure the host publishes hidden directories. Keep the original manifest URL available and update any existing service-worker cache so installed clients can receive the new files. Avoid blanket redirects: the old manifest page and `/transfer.html` must remain accessible.
+
+On the new site, **Copy saved settings** opens a first-party window on the old site. The user approves a transfer of allowlisted preferences through `postMessage`. Each message validates an exact origin, window reference and random per-attempt nonce. No settings enter a URL. Destination values win on conflicts, failed writes are rolled back where storage permits, and originals are never deleted. A blocked popup, denied storage or timeout leaves the copy retryable. Do not use `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` on these pages: it would sever the cross-origin opener needed by the handoff. Both origins must be trusted; any other deployment headers should retain this relationship.
+
+Supporting browsers offer an installation migration that retains OS integration after user approval. This cannot be guaranteed across browsers; the banner provides a visit/install fallback for browsers without origin migration. Settings can still be copied using the handoff. The app never tells a user to remove their original installation before copying their settings.
+
+The build follows the [manifest incubation specification](https://wicg.github.io/manifest-incubations/#migrate_to-member): `migrate_to` is an object, and the association key is the complete destination app ID (including its path). Some early guidance examples use a different shape.
+
+No development/preview server or browser verification is required to generate the bundles. A successful build checks configuration and produces files; it does not prove an installed browser's update behavior or a host's headers.
